I’ll first inventory the pattern, metadata, and reusable crystallography utilities, then identify chemically plausible phases before any cyclic refinement. I’ll keep candidate records and profile terms checkpointed so rejected trials can be rolled back cleanly.

The chemistry is a Ba–W–O reaction at 750 °C, so BaWO₄ is the primary exact-formula expectation, with residual WO₃/BaCO₃ and Ba-rich or W-rich tungstates as balance-plausible competitors. No precursor ratio is reported, so I’ll treat phase fractions as diffraction-derived rather than impose an invented mass-balance constraint. Next I’m extracting peak families and checking the local structural-record/GSAS-II tooling.

The peak inventory already anchors the main phase: the strongest independent families at about 26.69°, 28.39°, 32.04°, 43.19°, 48.98°, and 53.68° match scheelite BaWO₄. Several extra families near 23.40–24.65° make residual precursor or a secondary tungstate a real possibility, so I’m health-screening the exact BaWO₄ source and distinct WO₃, BaCO₃, Ba₃W₂O₉, and Ba₃WO₆ records before testing scales.

The “extra” peaks resolve into a coherent second-phase fingerprint, not an arbitrary collection of residuals: Ba₃W₂O₉ predicts independent lines at 16.22, 17.50, 25.98, 27.48, 30.55, 37.38, 40.52, 51.58, 62.81, 63.58, and 71.23°, each appearing with the same roughly +0.27° instrumental offset. That is much stronger evidence than the partial low-angle resemblance to WO₃/BaCO₃. I’m moving these two phases into the first quantitative fit while keeping the precursor polymorphs on the watch list.

The first adapted BaWO₄ files exposed an origin-setting trap during the mandatory health check: without explicit I4₁/a operators, the parser doubled the Ba and W multiplicities. I’m correcting the CIF symmetry explicitly before allowing either model into GSAS-II; this is exactly the kind of silent stoichiometry error the gate is meant to catch.

The first unconstrained start was deliberately rejected: with all peaks initially offset, the secondary scale was driven to its floor; releasing its cell afterward then collapsed the cell metric. Per the convergence gate, I’m restoring the entire neutral checkpoint and seeding only the independently measured displacement (~−460 µm from the matched peak families) before repeating scales/background. No values from that failed branch will be carried forward.

The common U/W/X trial improved Rwp but drove U negative, so it is not admissible under the requested nonnegative profile model. I’m reloading the saved W-only state and testing the complementary boundary model U = 0 with W+X active; the negative-U values are being discarded, not clipped in place.

Residual auditing now reveals a third coherent phase: the unmodeled 23.40/23.88/24.65° triplet and independent 33.56/34.45° lines match monoclinic WO₃ after the same displacement correction; the 24.17° line is separately explained by Ba₃W₂O₉. I’m comparing monoclinic, triclinic, orthorhombic, and alternate monoclinic WO₃ records from the same two-phase checkpoint before retaining a source.

The first source comparison strongly favors monoclinic P2₁/c WO₃ (COD 2311041): it lowers Rwp from 7.29% to 5.37% and retains a positive ~12.1 wt% fraction. The lower-symmetry and higher-symmetry competitors are still replaying from the same checkpoint so I can test whether that ranking survives cell refinement.

The source ranking is stable before and after cell refinement: monoclinic P2₁/c COD 2311041 gives Rwp = 5.37%, versus 5.80% for triclinic P−1 and 5.99% for orthorhombic Pnma. The lower-symmetry model does not beat the simpler monoclinic source. A second monoclinic file imported its charged atom labels incorrectly as hydrogen and produced nonsensical scales, so it is rejected as an unusable record rather than evidence against WO₃.

The three-phase neutral checkpoint is stable at Rwp = 5.367% with fractions near 72.3% BaWO₄, 15.7% Ba₃W₂O₉, and 12.1% WO₃. The remaining largest residuals are intensity/shape mismatches on already indexed families rather than a clean new phase fingerprint. I’m now running the required per-phase neutral/size/strain/combined broadening challenges from that single checkpoint.

The broadening matrix is still running because each branch is replayed to convergence rather than compared after one unstable cycle. All branches start from the same 20-variable checkpoint; I’m also recording whether any size/strain term returns to its neutral boundary, which would invalidate that apparent improvement.

The isotropic challenges separate cleanly:

- BaWO₄ size returned to the 10 µm neutral boundary, while strain became negative; both are rejected.
- Ba₃W₂O₉ supports finite broadening; size alone (≈0.34 µm) captures essentially all the combined gain without the extra strain term.
- WO₃ supports finite size broadening (≈0.10 µm); the combined trial again drove strain negative, so strain is rejected.

I’m combining only the two positive, replay-stable size terms and then checking whether symmetry-compatible anisotropy is actually warranted.

The anisotropy tests are small but coherent: Ba₃W₂O₉ prefers its symmetry axis [001] (about 0.24 µm equatorial vs 0.64 µm axial), and monoclinic WO₃ prefers its unique b axis [010] (about 0.10 vs 0.078 µm). Both improve χ² with stable fractions and positive dimensions; the alternate WO₃ axes are weaker. I’m combining those two terms, then comparing smooth background orders, polarization, and surface roughness before any texture term.

The shared-intensity tests are decisive but one is inadmissible: polarization runs to its upper bound of 1.0, so I’m rejecting that comparison. A one-parameter Suortti roughness term stays interior (B≈0.113), sharply improves Rwp to 4.03%, and leaves fractions stable; I’m finishing the two-parameter roughness replay to see whether A is independently supported.

Low-index texture challenges show three reproducible directions: BaWO₄ [112] is the dominant correction (MD≈1.24), while Ba₃W₂O₉ [110] and WO₃ [001] are smaller but independently resolved. Axial alternatives fit worse. I’m combining them now and will remove each in turn to ensure the gain is not merely being repartitioned among scale, roughness, and texture.

Removal challenges confirm the retained intensity terms: deleting BaWO₄ [112], WO₃ [001], or Ba₃W₂O₉ [110] worsens Rwp to 4.006%, 3.940%, and 3.932%, respectively, while deleting roughness worsens it to 5.001% and forces compensating texture/fraction shifts. The combined model is replay-stable at Rwp = 3.926%. I’m now repeating background and polarization challenges with these terms present.

With the accepted intensity terms present, order 8 is the last useful smooth-background increment (Rwp 3.9202%); orders 9–10 only add tiny coefficients and negligible gain. Polarization now refines back to 0.698, indistinguishable from the fixed 0.70 value, so it is pruned. I’m freezing the order-8 checkpoint and cross-seeding the competing BaWO₄ and WO₃ source models into both neutral and elaborated fits.

The source challenge has changed the structural ranking: the ordered SrWO₄-derived oxygen geometry (isovalent Sr→Ba, with the exact BaWO₄ cell) fits substantially better at the neutral checkpoint than the BaMoO₄-derived model, while the exact 1970 BaWO₄ record is penalized by its split half-occupied oxygen disorder. I’m letting the elaborated fits finish before switching, because source ranking must survive nuisance refinement.

The source ranking survives full elaboration and is unambiguous: the SrWO₄-derived ordered scheelite model gives Rwp = 3.751%, compared with 3.920% for the BaMoO₄-derived model and 4.644% for the split-oxygen exact record. Its BaWO₄ fraction is ~68.5% and the inherited [112] texture relaxes close to unity, showing that the better oxygen geometry—not stronger texture—is responsible. I’m switching to this model and repeating the convergence loop from the saved cross-seeded state.

The new-source replay exposes two previously retained terms as likely coupling: Ba₃W₂O₉ texture relaxes to 0.991 and WO₃’s two size dimensions become nearly equal. I’m therefore re-running the phase-width matrix and pruning any term that no longer survives the source change, rather than carrying it into the final fractions.

The source-adjusted width loop changes the physical model:

- BaWO₄ broadening is unsupported (size/strain gains are negligible).
- Ba₃W₂O₉ now clearly prefers isotropic microstrain (~1.27×10³ µstrain); combined size collapses to the 10 µm boundary and is invalid.
- WO₃ prefers a positive size+strain combination (~0.155 µm and ~2.17×10³ µstrain); its former uniaxial size split disappears and is pruned.

I’m combining those revised terms and then re-testing texture/roughness/background once more.

The phase inventory is now stable at BaWO₄ + Ba₃W₂O₉ + monoclinic WO₃, with all three supported by independent peak families. I’ve also replaced the initial BaWO₄ record with a health-screened scheelite model that fits substantially better; I’m now pruning texture/profile terms and re-challenging alternate sources and precursor phases before freezing the deliverables.

The latest replay gives \(R_\mathrm{wp}=3.719\%\) with provisional fractions 68.27/15.63/16.10 wt% (BaWO₄/Ba₃W₂O₉/WO₃). Those are not frozen yet: the inherited March–Dollase terms are being tested from a common no-texture checkpoint so that small intensity gains cannot silently bias the fractions.

Early texture removals are already showing that the three inherited corrections are weak in the new BaWO₄ source model—the profile changes only at the third decimal place. I’m finishing the axis-by-axis replay and will keep only terms that produce a reproducible, phase-specific improvement rather than a scale/background tradeoff.

The all-neutral texture model remains essentially as good as the textured one, while several trial axes converge only with heavy damping. That is a warning against retaining them. WO₃’s [001] correction is the only one showing a clearly larger removal penalty so far; I’ll verify that after the full loop and complementary geometry tests.

The texture-pruned checkpoint is stable at 68.32/15.58/16.10 wt% and \(R_\mathrm{wp}=3.719\%\). Background orders below seven are decisively worse; the ongoing loop is resolving whether the eighth coefficient and the surface-roughness term remain justified after the source and broadening changes.

Surface roughness is unquestionably required: removing it worsens the fit dramatically, while the alternative A-only form is singular and therefore invalid. Higher background orders give only very small incremental gains; I’m checking coefficient significance and replay stability before deciding between orders 8–10.

The two-parameter roughness model is not acceptable despite a lower residual: it needs prolonged damping, fails to replay-converge, and introduces a large unresolved shift/error. Per the reversible-loop rule I’m discarding that branch and retaining the single B term unless a clean replay contradicts it.

Polarization behaves like the rejected two-roughness branch: it is >98% correlated with scale/roughness and does not converge on replay. That branch is also being discarded; the measured Cu Kα polarization value stays fixed at 0.70.

A positive common \(U\) term survives replay (about 5σ) and modestly improves the fit, so it has been accepted. I’m now repeating the neutral/size/strain/combined comparisons for each phase from that exact checkpoint, including symmetry-compatible anisotropy challenges.

The first post-\(U\) results show strong profile coupling for BaWO₄: an apparent size gain is >98% correlated with the common \(X\) width, so it cannot be accepted as phase-specific. I’m letting the complementary strain/combined and other-phase trials finish before choosing the least coupled physical model.

Ba₃W₂O₉ still supports strain-only broadening; its combined size+strain trial becomes singular and is invalid. WO₃ clearly needs phase broadening, and its size/strain alternatives are being compared cleanly against a neutral model that is much worse.

The combined anisotropic model replays at \(R_\mathrm{wp}=3.699\%\). Both accepted anisotropies are now being removed one at a time; WO₃’s removal penalty is large, while adding a second anisotropic size term appears to provide no independent gain and is likely to be pruned.

With the accepted width model, order 7 is clearly inadequate; order 9 gives a small but reproducible improvement over order 8. I’m extending the same smooth series through order 12 to identify the plateau instead of selecting the lowest residual automatically.

The polarization cross-check is exposing the expected coupling: fixing roughness gives one polarization value, while fixing polarization changes the optimized roughness and improves the fit differently. I’m finishing the both-free and no-roughness replays; unless the value/fractions are invariant across those seeds, polarization will remain fixed.

The smooth-background series plateaus after order 11; the 12th term is only ~2.3σ and shifts fractions more than it helps, so order 11 is retained. Polarization is rejected: freeing it with roughness drives polarization above 1 and roughness negative, while fixed-term cross-seeds give incompatible optima.

The retained chemistry is now being re-challenged from the fully elaborated checkpoint: two BaCO₃ polymorphs/records, disordered and ordered Ba₃WO₆ models, and extra triclinic/orthorhombic WO₃ competitors. Each starts from two independent scales with neutral broadening and texture.

The final watch-list challenge is still running. One carbonate candidate initially produced a large fit change, so I’m checking its refined scale sign and whether the improvement corresponds to real independent peaks rather than a cancellation artifact before freezing the inventory.

The carbonate result is real enough to promote: orthorhombic BaCO₃ converged from both scale seeds to about 5 wt% and cut Rwp from 3.686% to 3.282%; the alternate orthorhombic and hexagonal records collapsed to zero. I’m now reopening its cell and running the same broadening/texture/background challenges. A nominal ~1.1 wt% second WO₃ polymorph also appeared, but I’m treating that as provisional because same-formula splitting must survive much stricter replacement and line-shape tests.

The carbonate cell refined cleanly to 5.3030 × 8.9152 × 6.4293 Å (Pmcn), and its fraction remained 5.13 wt%. That also exposed an important coupling: the earlier WO₃ texture correction relaxed almost to unity, so I will re-prune it rather than carrying it forward by inertia. The carbonate line-width loop is now comparing neutral, size, strain, combined, and symmetry-compatible anisotropic models from the same checkpoint.

The isotropic carbonate strain term is physically stable and improves the fit; the combined size-plus-strain and some anisotropic trials become singular or drive the cell metric invalid on replay, so those comparisons are disqualified under the reversible-loop rule. I’m letting the remaining axes finish, then I’ll reload the stable isotropic checkpoint and challenge texture and common-profile terms from there.

The post-carbonate loop confirms the Ba₃W₂O₉ broadening is essential. BaWO₄ now shows a possible isotropic-strain gain, but it is strongly correlated with both common U and X, so I’m withholding acceptance until the complementary common-profile challenges show that it is phase-specific rather than another shared-width surrogate.

The broadening inventory has settled: Ba₃W₂O₉ retains its [001] strain anisotropy; WO₃ simplifies to isotropic size plus isotropic strain; BaWO₄ stays neutral because its apparent strain is negative and its size is 98.5% correlated with the common X width. The texture sweep now starts from all March ratios fixed at one.

No texture term survives the replayed sweep: the best nominal gain was only about four χ² units for one parameter and did not recur across phase families, while all fractions were unchanged. I have therefore fixed every March ratio at one and am now selecting the final smooth background/profile geometry from that neutral checkpoint.

The no-roughness branch is now explicitly invalid: it becomes singular, worsens χ² by roughly 60%, and cannot reconverge from the common checkpoint. I am preserving the accepted B-only branch; the slow failed replay is isolated in its own GPX and cannot contaminate later trials.

The joint polarization/roughness branch again chases a large numerical gain through a very slow, coupled trajectory. I’m letting the isolated replay finish so its final physical bounds and correlations are recorded; it will only be accepted if both parameters remain physical and the result replays cleanly.

The joint polarization/roughness trial is rejected despite its lower Rwp: after two 30-cycle passes it still reports nonconvergence, a 4.26σ maximum shift, −96.4% polarization–roughness correlation, and >98% correlations with the dominant phase scale. The independently fixed-B polarization trial improves only the high-angle segment while worsening low-angle and isolated phase families. I’m retaining fixed polarization 0.7, B-only roughness, and the parsimonious 10-term background.

The rebuilt watch list is behaving consistently so far: the alternate carbonate polymorphs and both Ba₃WO₆ models collapse at the nonnegative scale floor from independent starts. I’m waiting on the two WO₃ coexistence tests, which are the only remaining topology question.

The weak triclinic-WO₃ scale is positive from both starts, but that alone is insufficient. Its dedicated challenge is testing whether the ~0.62 wt% survives reallocating size/strain and low-index texture between the two WO₃ models; any unstable fraction split or bound width will exclude it from the submitted inventory.

The coexistence test is already showing the warning sign expected for an over-split phase: adding broadening to the triclinic component makes one width parameter singular, while giving the monoclinic component anisotropic strain produces essentially no additional gain. I’m completing the cell/texture challenges to see whether the small triclinic fraction remains numerically stable.

The replacement loop hit a GSAS-II scripting cache issue after safely deleting the old phase from a copied project; no accepted checkpoint was affected. I’m fixing the copied project’s phase-name cache and rerunning the source comparisons from their common checkpoint.

The lower-symmetry WO₃ replacement is decisively worse on the final profile (χ² roughly doubles before its elaboration becomes singular), so it cannot replace the monoclinic model. I’m stopping further invalid width elaboration on that rejected branch and will run the remaining BaWO₄ source comparison separately.

The inventory decision is now stable: I will submit four phases. The provisional triclinic WO₃ component is excluded because its fraction ranges from about 0.39 to 0.82 wt% under legitimate texture/broadening reallocations and one width trial becomes singular; that fails the same-formula coexistence gate despite a small Rwp gain. The monoclinic WO₃ model remains the physically supported single-source model.

The accepted four-phase project has now replayed three times over the complete 9.903–100.095° range with Rwp 3.17957%, GOF 1.05619, and maximum shift/esd 0.00024. I’ve exported the phase CIFs and am running the required final geometry health screen before writing the report and validating cross-file consistency.

The first cross-file audit found only a CSV serialization issue: ten significant digits caused a maximum 3.9×10⁻⁶ count rounding discrepancy in `observed − calculated`. The scientific model is unchanged; I’m increasing profile precision and rerunning the audit so the CSV and GPX agree numerically at machine-useful precision.

Final validation now passes: all four CIFs pass the geometry/occupancy health screen; `result.json` fractions sum exactly to 1.0; all 8,301 `fit.csv` rows agree with `final.gpx`; and the reported Rwp matches the project exactly.

Completed with four modeled crystalline phases:

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| BaWO₄ | 64.64% |
| Ba₃W₂O₉ | 14.99% |
| Monoclinic WO₃ | 12.64% |
| Orthorhombic BaCO₃ | 7.73% |

Final fit: Rwp 3.1796%, Rp 2.4566%, GOF 1.0562. Fractions are normalized over crystalline phases. BaCO₃ has greater systematic uncertainty, approximately 5–8 wt% across plausible line-shape models. A tentative trace triclinic WO₃ component was excluded because its fraction was not replay-stable.

